--TEST--
A short attribute name that both the owner chain and the namespace or a `use` import give does not compile, and the error names both declarations, while each qualified form compiles — `rule:attributes/an-attribute-is-declared-with-its-sites`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:attributes/an-attribute-is-declared-with-the-places-it-may-be-used
namespace Shop;

use Core\Deprecated;

// `Index` is declared in `Storable` and in the namespace `Shop`.
// `Deprecated` is declared in `Storable` and imported from `Core`.
abstract class Storable {
    attribute Index on class { columns: string }
    attribute Deprecated on method { reason: string }
}
attribute Index on class { columns: string }

// Each qualified name names one declaration, so this class compiles.
#[Storable::Index(columns: "email")]
#[Shop\Index(columns: "id")]
class Accepted extends Storable {
    #[Storable::Deprecated(reason: "use find")]
    public function load(): int {
        return 1;
    }
}

// Inside a descendant of `Storable`, each short name below is given by two scopes.
#[Index(columns: "email")]
class Refused extends Storable {
    #[Deprecated(reason: "use find")]
    public function load(): int {
        return 1;
    }
}

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E1007]: `Index` is declared in `Shop\Storable` and in `Shop`, so write `Storable::Index` or `Shop\Index`
   --> case.nvs:26:3
%A
error[E1007]: `Deprecated` is declared in `Shop\Storable` and in `Core`, so write `Storable::Deprecated` or `Core\Deprecated`
   --> case.nvs:28:7
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
